  "name": "tafoni-sandstone",
  "notes": "Cavernous weathering: a pit holds moisture and salt, so it deepens faster than the rock around it. The runaway pitting is what hollows out coastal and desert sandstone. Cavernous alone, though, retreats the RIM along with the floor and leaves a smooth bowl; what makes a hollow read as tafoni is a case-hardened skin that survives around the opening while the soft rock behind it scoops out. Hence rind, at half the strength honeycomb-tafoni uses - this is the larger, bedded sandstone block with a few deep hollows, not a honeycombed face.",
